  "context_pill": "Capital Gains on Amalgamation",
  "example_summary": "M's shares in ABC Ltd were replaced by shares in an amalgamated Indian company, resulting in a market value increase of ₹ 50,000 which the AO tried to tax.",
  "source_provision": "| Statutory Provision | Core Condition / Eligibility | Tax Treatment & Legal Consequence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Section 47(vii)** | Transfer of shares held in amalgamating company by a shareholder in consideration of allotment of shares in the amalgamated Indian company. | **Not regarded as transfer**, hence exempt from Capital Gains tax u/s 45. |\n| **Section 49(2)** | Cost of acquisition of shares in the amalgamated company shall be deemed to be the cost of acquisition of shares in the amalgamating company. | **Cost Carry Forward**: Cost and holding period of old shares are substituted into the new shares. |\n| **Section 2(42A)** | Period of holding of shares in the amalgamated company includes the period for which the shares in the amalgamating company were held. | **Holding Period Integration**: Determines whether capital gains are Long-Term (LTCG) or Short-Term (STCG). |\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Amalgamation Condition: The amalgamated company **must be an Indian company** for Section 47(vii) exemption to apply.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Assessing Officer Error: Treating the allotment of higher market value shares as a taxable **exchange** is legally incorrect when statutory conditions of amalgamation are satisfied.",
